{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 29, :comments ["<b>And he mentioned</b> the name of Nahor's wife to make known the lineage of Rebekah, Rachel, and Leah. Our Sages, of blessed memory, said that Iscah is Sarah; if it is a tradition, we will accept it. Those who say that Abraham was infertile and not Sarah say the opposite of what is written, and the witness is Ishmael his son, and the sons of Keturah. Those who say that Sarah was Abraham's sister — this reason is not right in my eyes. If it were so, Scripture would have said, 'And Terah took Abram his son and Sarai his daughter, wife of Abram his son.' Likewise, if she were Lot's sister, Scripture would have said 'and Sarai daughter of his son,' as it wrote concerning Lot. It is possible that his son Nahor went first to Haran, or came after his father. The correct view in my eyes is that the section 'Go forth' (Genesis 12:1) — which HaShem said to Abram — preceded this verse, which is 'And Terah took,' and its like is in the Torah: 'On the first day of the second month in the second year' (Numbers 1:1), and afterward, 'In the first month in the second year' (Exodus 40:17). And the proof is that it is written 'to go to the land of Canaan' (Genesis 11:31), and when Terah came to Haran, the place was right in his eyes, and he dwelt there and died. And there is no earlier and later in the Torah."], :book :genesis, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 11, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [["On Your Way" "http://mobile.tora.ws/"] ["Piotrkow, 1907-1911" "https://www.nli.org.il/he/books/NNL_ALEPH990020973480205171/NLI"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T18:44:17.617232978Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}